nodecode-document-ocr is a rewrite of lEWFkRAD's lEWFkRAD/hermes-document-reader:

  https://github.com/lEWFkRAD/hermes-document-reader
  commit f3a0bed28e6c289942b899924d9e7cf8b93045e8
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Jeff Robodie (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the OCR wire of engine/grm_ocr.py -- an OpenAI-compatible
multimodal endpoint called with one page image as a base64 data URL, the
thinking flag turned off, a transcription instruction asking for
layout-preserving Markdown (chandra's ocr_layout prompt is replaced by one
written for this cell), the endpoint's /models probe, HTTPS unless the
endpoint is loopback or the operator opts out, and a bounded per-call
budget -- as the Common Lisp the model and the operator call: the tools
document_ocr and document_ocr_probe and the command /document-ocr. A PDF is
rendered page by page with the pdftoppm of poppler when the user has it,
which stands in for the original's own page rendering. Everything lives in
one Lisp file; nothing of the original's Python is bundled or run.

Left out: the Windows-only profile service and its scheduled task, the
watched inbox folder and its processed / on-hold / needs-review / quarantine
trees, the job history and preview cache, the desktop reader window and HUD
attachment action, the loopback HTTP API the reader talks to, the .xlsx
workbook export (the recognized text is returned, and written as .md/.txt
when the caller names a file), the repeat-token retry and the model-output
HTML sanitiser, since this cell answers text to a model rather than drawing
pages in a window.
